% PowerPoint slides 2.
### How do we Implement a Parser?
[review] @(84, 165) {$note, color=emp} A quick review!
...
[step1] @(84, 218) {$note} **Step 1: Design the grammar.** Handle exponents and parentheses, as in `(2^3)^2`.
...
[original] @(84, 298) {$code, w=560px, size=26px} ```
Expr → Term '^' Expr | Term
Term → num | '(' Expr ')'
```
...
[conflict] @(700, 301) {$note, w=490px} Both Expr options start with {{color=math|Term}}.{{br}}The next token cannot tell us which to pick.
...
[assoc] @(700, 414) {$note, w=490px} `^` is {{color=emp|right-associative}}.
...
[step2] @(84, 472) {$note} **Step 2: Hack your grammar.** Defer the choice until lookahead can decide.
...
[factored] @(84, 534) {$code, size=26px} ```
Expr → Term ExprExtra
ExprExtra → '^' Expr | ε
Term → num | '(' Expr ')'
```
---
% PowerPoint slides 3–4.
### How do we Implement a Parser?
[review] @(84, 160) {$note, color=emp} A quick review
[named] @(84, 210) {$code, w=615px, size=25px} ```
Expr → Term ExprExtra
ExprExtra → '^' Expr | ε
Term → num | '(' Expr ')'
```
...
[short] @(752, 210) {$code, w=444px, size=25px} ```
S → T S′
S′ → '^' S | ε
T → num | '(' S ')'
```
...
[predict] @(84, 371) {$table, size=24px, pad=10px, colwidths=(28%,16%,14%,16%,13%,13%)} |  | num | ^ | ( | ) | EOF |
|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|:---|
| Expr (S) | → T S′ |  | → T S′ |  |  |
| ExprExtra (S′) |  | → ^ S |  | → ε | → ε |
| Term (T) | → num |  | → ( S ) |  |  |
...
[functions] @(84, 599) {$note} We need three functions:{{br}}`ParseExpr()`, `ParseExprExtra()`, and `ParseTerm()`.
% EOF is the end-of-input marker shown as $ in the source table. Blank cells are errors.
