% PowerPoint slides 11.
### How do we build our AST?
[categories] @(84, 170) {$table, size=25px, pad=16px, colwidths=(16%,38%,46%)} | Node type | When used? | Examples |
|:---|:---|:---|
| {{color=emp|Leaf}} | Tokens that need no inputs | Variables or literals |
| {{color=neon_pink|Internal}} | Important production rules | Plus, assign, if, print |
| {{color=id|Skipped}} | Direct transitions or syntax not needed later | Parentheses; var declarations; `Expr3 → Expr2` |
...
[leaf] @(84, 490) {$note} **Leaves** supply values. **Internal nodes** describe operations.
...
[skip] @(84, 572) {$note} For a skipped rule, pass its useful subtree up to the parent.
% Declaration information may be stored in the symbol table; retain any initializer work.
% The examples preserve the categories in the course slides.
---
% PowerPoint slides 12.
### What sort of data structure?
We have two basic options:
...
- A {{color=emp|single data structure}} containing all the information we might need.
...
- A {{color=emp|base class}} with subclasses for each possible node type.
...
The first is quicker to implement.
...
The second will serve you better in the long term.
---
% PowerPoint slides 13.
### Structures in an AST
What are the leaves of the abstract syntax tree?
...
- A literal number.
...
- A variable.
...
- Commands or functions with no arguments.
...
- Strings, in the future.
...
A grammar rule with only terminals on its RHS will {{color=emp|typically produce a leaf}}.
...
These are the first rules reduced in a bottom-up parse.
---
